/*
 *+
 * FUNCTION NAME:
 * motorIntHandler
 *
 * INVOCATION:
 * motorIntHandler(who)
 *
 * PARAMETERS: (">" input, "!" modified, "<" output)
 * (>) who (int)            Which card interrupted
 *
 * FUNCTION VALUE:
 * (void) Returns 'void' and hence has no STATUS value
 *
 * PURPOSE:
 * Handles interrupts from the OMS motor control cards.
 *
 * DESCRIPTION:
 * An interrupt has been received.
 *
 * First, check the status register.  If there are errors, arrange to have
 * the register cleared later.  Count overtravel as it is not fatal in
 * this application (could be ignored).  If the commands sent to the
 * hardware caused trouble, arrange for the next status check (getMdStatus)
 * to print the command sequence leading up to (and including) the
 * error.
 *
 * If there is a DONE flag set in the done register, and there is a task
 * waiting on that flag, signal the task by giving the semDone semaphore.
 *
 * If the transmit buffer is empty (MD_TBE_S set in the status register),
 * a charcter may be sent.  If the status register needs to be cleared
 * of an error, send that character in preference to any other.  Otherwise,
 * get the next character from the output ring buffer.  If there are no
 * characters to send, disable the transmitter empty interrupt.
 *
 * Finally, is there a character available to be read from the controller?
 * If so, store it in the message area.  A small state machine keeps track
 * of various LF and CR characters, discarding them while using their
 * presence to detect start and end of the responses.  It's quite
 * simple, maybe clever, but also fragile as there's no way to recover
 * from an error.  At the end of the message, the semResponse semaphore
 * is given to signal that the message is available; only one task is allowed
 * to wait for a controller response message at a time.  Of course, all
 * four axes can be waiting for DONE flags.
 *
 * EXTERNAL VARIABLES:
 *
 *
 * PRIOR REQUIREMENTS:
 *
 *
 * DEFICIENCIES:
 *
 *
 * HISTORY:
 * Jul 1, 2001      Initial Version                 (rwolff@noao.edu)
 *
 *-
 */
/*
 *+
 * FUNCTION NAME:
 * testHardware
 *
 * INVOCATION:
 * testHardware()
 *
 * PARAMETERS: (">" input, "!" modified, "<" output)
 * None
 *
 * FUNCTION VALUE:
 * (int) Returns VME_OK (0) or VME_ERROR (1) as status
 *
 * PURPOSE:
 * Test hardware for proper functioning
 *
 * DESCRIPTION:
 * This command is intended to implement the Gemini 'test' command.
 * It is a requirement that this command not cause any change of state
 * of the system.  So the tests check for proper behavior for commands
 * that typically request the hardware ID string; if the hardware is
 * missing, or in the some gross way, not functioning, this test will
 * fail.  This is, however, not a test for subtle problems.
 *
 * For the motors, it is assumed that the motors are not in operation
 * when the test is requested (that would be foolish).  So turning the
 * motor off is an acceptable null test (and judging from the software,
 * it really is almost a total NOP, ie., pointless).
 *
 *
 * EXTERNAL VARIABLES:
 *
 *
 * PRIOR REQUIREMENTS:
 *
 *
 * DEFICIENCIES:
 *
 *
 * HISTORY:
 * Jul 1, 2001      Initial Version                 (rwolff@noao.edu)
 *
 *-
 */
